# Prisma 7 Type Handling

## Context
- When working with Prisma models in TypeScript files
- When importing types from the generated Prisma Client
- When defining component props or state variables that use Prisma models

## Requirements

- NEVER import from `@prisma/client` — always from the generated output path
- Platform: `import { PrismaClient } from '../generated/prisma/client'` (relative to `packages/auth/`)
- Intranet: `import { PrismaClient } from '../generated/prisma/client'` (relative to repo root)
- Other packages: `import type { User } from '../../../auth/generated/prisma/client'`
- ALWAYS use `type` keyword for type-only imports: `import type { User } from '...'`
- ALWAYS use `PrismaPg` adapter when creating PrismaClient instances
- Use local interfaces when imports fail
- After schema changes: run `npm run db:generate` to regenerate client

## Examples

<example>
✅ Prisma 7 correct usage:
```typescript
import { PrismaClient } from '../generated/prisma/client';
import { PrismaPg } from '@prisma/adapter-pg';

const adapter = new PrismaPg({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL! });
const prisma = new PrismaClient({ adapter });
```
</example>

<example>
✅ Type-only import:
```typescript
import type { User, Session } from '../generated/prisma/client';
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
❌ Old Prisma 6 pattern (broken in v7):
```typescript
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
const prisma = new PrismaClient();
```
</example>

<critical>
- NEVER import from `@prisma/client` — use the generated output path
- NEVER create PrismaClient without a driver adapter
- ALWAYS run `npm run db:generate` after schema changes
</critical>
